Z||000000||DJRice||07-19-99||11:29 AM||||Problems with a fuel pump.....<p>I am replacing the blown 4 cyl from my 84 with a very nicely kept 4 cyl from an 85.  The donor car was a real steal too.  I bought it off of a guy for $200us.  He thought the transmission was screwed, because after he replaced the shifter cable, he still only had one gear (2nd). So got the car to my house, pulled the console off to inspect his cable replacement job, saw that this dude did not bother to replace the shifter cable retaining rings and smiled in delight.  Installation of a retaining ring restored operation of all 5 gears.  This car had been sitting for about 3 years, and the engine runs like a top, but the interior was trashed along with some unhappy dings on the outside.  So I decided this would make a good donor for my 84.  Anyway...I replaced cars main wiring harness from front to back and I am using the 85 ecm.<p>The stupid fuel pump does not run.  If I hot wire it, every thing goes, but I want to fix it right.  So, my question is this.  Does the  oil pressure sending unit operate parallel to the fuel pump relay or is it a backup to the relay.  Haynes manual makes it sound like a backup.  If it is not the relay or the oil pressure sender, where else should I look?<br>Could I have missed a ground?<p>I thought I understood this fuel system well, but I cant seem to make this work.  Help....
Z||000001||theogre||07-19-99||12:10 PM||||the ECM controls the fuel pump durring startting. The Oil switch keeps it on after that. The contacts are wired in parallel as far as I can see.
